s\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"cracked_solar_array\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"dented_fuel_bladder\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"foil_insulation_bale\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"collapsed_antenna_dish\",\n      \"verdict\": \"revise\",\n      \"issues\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"contradicts_gdd\",\n          \"quote\": \"still wider than your cockpit\",\n          \"evidence\": \"[4.3] \\\"Size classes, fragile flag, compactor, crane magnet, oversized junk. All exist to serve the endgame. Every piece is one slot; the module is simply very heavy.\\\"\",\n          \"cites\": [\"4.3\"],\n          \"why\": \"Raising this once, on this item, as the representative: §4.3 lists size classes, the fragile flag and oversized junk as cut from scope, and the loot table still carries all three (this row is flagged oversized; six rows are flagged FRAGILE). The collision is between the document and the table, not inside the writing — the table is what the game will spawn, so every size and fragility read in this batch stands, including this one. Nothing to correct here; it is a finding about the two sources disagreeing.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"type\": \"contradicts_gdd\",\n          \"quote\": \"and it eats the whole slot doing it\",\n          \"evidence\": \"[4.3] \\\"Every piece is one slot; the module is simply very heavy.\\\"\",\n          \"cites\": [\"4.3\"],\n          \"why\": \"This goes past the table's size class into cargo-slot economy. The table says oversized; it does not say oversized pieces consume capacity differently, and §4.3 positively states every piece is one slot — including this one. Bulk is fair to describe; a slot cost is not.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"corrected\": {\n        \"flavour\": \"Folded in on itself like a dropped umbrella and still wider than your cockpit. It hangs where the scrap is cheap, and it is all elbows on the tether the whole way down.\"\n      }\n    },\n    { \"id\": \"reaction_wheel_housing\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"snapped_boom_arm\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"shattered_camera_mast\",\n      \"verdict\": \"revise\",\n      \"issues\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"invented_mechanic\",\n          \"quote\": \"or you'll be selling gravel from the bargain end of the band\",\n          \"evidence\": \"[5d] \\\"Satellite → Apollo-era module that degrades (pristine → damaged → scrap) instead of being destroyed, and its condition selects the ending and scales payout.\\\"\",\n          \"cites\": [\"5d\"],\n          \"why\": \"Selling the broken piece at a reduced rate is a payout that scales with condition. The passages define that mechanic for Armstrong's module alone, and define it by contrast — the module degrades *instead of being destroyed*, which is what ordinary debris does. A fragile piece may read as delicate and may be lost; it may not have a damaged resale price.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"corrected\": {\n        \"flavour\": \"The optics head went first and the mast is holding its pieces together out of habit. Snatch it on the tether and there is nothing left to bring down — and this low, there was never much to bring down anyway.\"\n      }\n    },\n    { \"id\": \"battery_pallet\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"torn_radiator_panel\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"cryo_tank_girdle\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"gyro_package\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"docking_collar\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"buckled_truss_node\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"optics_bench\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"deployable_mast_cassette\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"ion_thruster_cluster\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"cracked_pressure_dome\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"reactor_shield_plug\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"comsat_bus_frame\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"telescope_mirror_segment\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    { \"id\": \"isotope_cask\", \"verdict\": \"pass\", \"issues\": [] },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"solar_wing_spine\",\n      \"verdict\": \"revise\",\n      \"issues\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"number_disagrees\",\n          \"quote\": \"it takes most of a tank to bring home\",\n          \"evidence\": \"[2.6a] \\\"a ballistic arc never pays for circularisation, which is most of the delta-v, but it comes back in faster and hotter and gives a short working window; circularising costs the rest of the tank and buys time.\\\"\",\n          \"cites\": [\"2.6a\"],\n          \"why\": \"The only tank arithmetic the passages give is routing: circularisation is most of the delta-v and costs the rest of the tank. Nothing in the passages or the table prices a single piece of cargo in fuel, so \\\"most of a tank\\\" is a quantity neither source supports. §2.6a does back the qualitative version — the good stuff \\\"fights you on the tether and on the way down.\\\"\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"corrected\": {\n        \"flavour\": \"The full backbone of an array wing, panels stripped, still longer than your ship. It comes off the rich end of the band and it fights you on the tether and the whole way down.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"scorched_reentry_cone\",\n      \"verdict\": \"revise\",\n      \"issues\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"contradicts_gdd\",\n          \"quote\": \"The heaviest thing on the board, the highest hanging\",\n          \"evidence\": \"[2.6a] \\\"At the very top hangs Armstrong's module: Apollo-era hardware that predates — and outlived — the cascade.\\\" / [4.3] \\\"Every piece is one slot; the module is simply very heavy.\\\"\",\n          \"cites\": [\"2.6a\", \"4.3\"],\n          \"why\": \"Both superlatives belong to Armstrong's module. The table does settle superlatives among its own rows — 1600 kg at 276,000 m is the heaviest and highest row it carries — but the passages put the module above everything in the band and describe it as very heavy, so \\\"the highest hanging\\\" and \\\"the heaviest thing on the board\\\" are claims about the field, and the field has the module in it. This also steps on the module's whole job: the glint overhead that the player is not ready for yet.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"corrected\": {\n        \"flavour\": \"Something big came down wearing this once and it is charred through, the ablative crumbling wherever you grip. The heaviest thing you can get a tether on short of the module itself, and the one most likely to come apart on the way to the deck.\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":122186,"ttft_stream_ms":1428,"time_to_request_ms":362,"type":"result","duration_ms":146575,"uuid":"04148685-b4d3-4cb7-9523-0ebe254cd1d3"}
